{%- comment -%}
  Easy Recommendation — app embed.

  Publishes runtime config for reco.js, and — on a product page carrying an
  offer — the offer itself plus the script to render it.

  The script used to be left to the blocks, which declare it through their schema
  `javascript` key. That was fine while the blocks did all the rendering, but it
  meant a product page with no block never loaded reco.js at all, so an offer the
  embed had inlined had nothing to draw it. It is loaded here as well now; the
  runtime bails if it has already run, so a page with both costs one cached
  request.

  reco.js reads the config object at call time rather than load time, so the two
  can arrive in either order.
{%- endcomment -%}

<script>
  window.EasyReco = window.EasyReco || {};
  window.EasyReco.config = {
    proxy: "/apps/easy-reco",
    shop: {{ shop.permanent_domain | json }},
    moneyFormat: {{ shop.money_format | strip_html | json }},
    enabled: {% if block.settings.enable_tracking %}true{% else %}false{% endif %},
    strings: {
      addToCart: {{ 'recommendations.add_to_cart' | t | json }},
      added: {{ 'recommendations.added' | t | json }},
      soldOut: {{ 'recommendations.sold_out' | t | json }},
      chooseOptions: {{ 'recommendations.choose_options' | t | json }},
      error: {{ 'recommendations.error' | t | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        The injected offer builds its own slider arrows, so their labels come from
        here rather than from a block's Liquid. reco.js falls back to English if
        the embed is off — but this path *is* the embed, so it never has to.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      previous: {{ 'recommendations.previous' | t | json }},
      next: {{ 'recommendations.next' | t | json }},
      count: {{ 'recommendations.count' | t | json }},
      countdown: {{ 'recommendations.countdown' | t | json }},
      countdownDays: {{ 'recommendations.countdown_days' | t | json }},
      quantity: {{ 'recommendations.quantity' | t | json }}
    }
  };
</script>

{%- comment -%}
  The offer for this product, inlined for the app embed to render.

  This is what makes the embed path work without a theme block. The embed runs on
  every page, so on a product template it can read that product's own
  `$app:reco_overrides` metafield — the same mirror the theme block reads (§3.1) —
  and hand reco.js everything it needs.

  Products are resolved here in Liquid rather than fetched by reco.js: the prices
  are then formatted by the shop's own rules, the currency is right, and there is
  no network hop at all. `all_products` is capped at 20 lookups per page and an
  override holds at most 12, so this stays clear of the ceiling.

  The shape deliberately matches Shopify's Ajax product JSON, because renderFallback
  already knows how to draw that — no second renderer.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if template.name == 'product' and product -%}
  {%- liquid
    comment
      Two places an offer can come from, in this order:

        1. This product's own `$app:reco_overrides` — a list the merchant curated
           for this product, or a "specific products" offer published onto it.
        2. `$app:reco_offers` on the shop — every published offer whose trigger is
           "all products" or a collection (CLAUDE.md 7.8). The trigger is matched
           here, against the product in front of us, which is what makes "all
           products" include products added after the offer was published.

      An **offer** published onto this product wins: a merchant who named this product
      meant this product, and a catalogue-wide offer must not overrule that.

      A list curated on the recommendations page does **not** win, and this is the
      distinction that was wrong. The condition used to be "does this product have any
      items in its metafield", so a curated list — which has no offer behind it and is
      therefore never injected (7.9) — shadowed the shop list and then rendered nothing
      itself. On a store with curated lists, an "all products" offer went missing on
      exactly the products that already had one, which is the hardest possible version
      of the bug to see.

      `offerId` is what separates the two: only a publish writes it.
    endcomment
    assign reco = product.metafields["$app"].reco_overrides.value

    if reco.offerId == blank
      assign shop_offers = shop.metafields["$app"].reco_offers.value.offers

      for candidate_offer in shop_offers
        comment
          Trigger: all products, or this product being in one of the collections
          the offer names. Handles rather than ids — a handle is what
          `product.collections` can be compared to without a lookup per page.
        endcomment
        assign matches = false
        if candidate_offer.trigger.mode == 'all'
          assign matches = true
        elsif candidate_offer.trigger.mode == 'collections'
          for product_collection in product.collections
            if candidate_offer.trigger.collections contains product_collection.handle
              assign matches = true
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif

        comment
          Exclusions are checked after the trigger and win over it: they exist to
          carve pages out of a match, so an excluded product is never a match even
          when the trigger says every product.
        endcomment
        if matches
          assign product_id_string = product.id | append: ''
          if candidate_offer.exclude.products contains product_id_string
            assign matches = false
          else
            for product_collection in product.collections
              if candidate_offer.exclude.collections contains product_collection.handle
                assign matches = false
                break
              endif
            endfor
          endif
        endif

        if matches
          comment
            The first match wins, and the list arrives oldest-published first — so
            an offer set up earlier keeps its pages when a later, broader one is
            added.
          endcomment
          assign reco = candidate_offer
          break
        endif
      endfor
    endif

    comment
      An automated offer has no items by design: Shopify supplies the list in the
      browser, so there is nothing to resolve in Liquid and reco.js fetches it.
    endcomment
    assign automated = false
    if reco.source.mode == 'automated'
      assign automated = true
    endif

    comment
      The embed injects **offers**, and nothing else.

      The same `$app:reco_overrides` metafield also holds lists curated on the
      recommendations page, which have no offer behind them. Injecting those made a
      widget appear on a product page the moment the app embed was enabled, with the
      admin showing no offers at all — which is not what enabling an embed asks for.

      Those lists still render wherever the merchant *places* a theme block: they
      chose that. What they never chose is a block appearing on its own.

      `offerId` is the per-product shape, `id` the shop-list one.
    endcomment
    assign offer_id = reco.offerId
    if offer_id == blank
      assign offer_id = reco.id
    endif

    comment
      Spelled out rather than written as `a and b or c and d` — Liquid has no operator
      precedence and evaluates a mixed and/or chain right to left, so that form says
      something other than it reads. There is a test for exactly this shape.

      Renderable means: an offer stands behind this data, and there is something to
      draw — a resolved list, or an automated source that will fetch one.
    endcomment
    assign renderable_offer = false
    if offer_id != blank
      if reco.items != blank
        assign renderable_offer = true
      elsif automated
        assign renderable_offer = true
      endif
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if renderable_offer -%}
    {%- capture offer_items -%}
      {%- assign rendered = 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        `hide_trigger` defaults to true — a product has never been offered as its
        own recommendation, and a v1 metafield with no visibility block must keep
        behaving that way.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign hide_trigger = true -%}
      {%- if reco.visibility.hideTrigger == false or reco.visibility.hideTrigger == 'false' -%}
        {%- assign hide_trigger = false -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- for entry in reco.items -%}
        {%- assign candidate = all_products[entry.handle] -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Spelled out rather than written as `a and b or c` — Liquid has no operator
          precedence and evaluates a mixed and/or chain right to left, so that form
          says something other than it reads.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign include_candidate = false -%}
        {%- if candidate.id -%}
          {%- if candidate.id != product.id -%}
            {%- assign include_candidate = true -%}
          {%- elsif hide_trigger == false -%}
            {%- assign include_candidate = true -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Out of stock never ships. The Offer tab says out loud that "only offer
          items that are in stock will be displayed on product pages", and the card
          would otherwise render with a disabled Sold out button — a recommendation
          the shopper cannot act on, sitting where a buyable one could be.

          This is the one place the offer path and the theme block differ on
          availability: a block the merchant placed keeps Sold out, because that is
          the documented behaviour of its own settings (§8 deviations).
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if include_candidate and candidate.available == false -%}
          {%- assign include_candidate = false -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if include_candidate -%}
          {%- if rendered > 0 %},{% endif -%}
          {
            "id": {{ candidate.id | json }},
            "handle": {{ candidate.handle | json }},
            "title": {{ candidate.title | json }},
            "url": {{ candidate.url | json }},
            "price": {{ candidate.price | json }},
            "compare_at_price": {{ candidate.compare_at_price | json }},
            "available": {{ candidate.available | json }},
            "featured_image": {%- if candidate.featured_image -%}
              {{ candidate.featured_image | image_url: width: 400 | json }}
            {%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
            "variants": [
              {%- for variant in candidate.variants limit: 20 -%}
                {%- unless forloop.first %},{% endunless -%}
                {
                  "id": {{ variant.id | json }},
                  "title": {{ variant.title | json }},
                  "price": {{ variant.price | json }},
                  "available": {{ variant.available | json }}
                }
              {%- endfor -%}
            ]
          }
          {%- assign rendered = rendered | plus: 1 -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      An automated offer renders from a fetch, so it needs no resolved items — but a
      *specific* offer whose products have all been deleted has nothing to draw and
      must not emit an empty widget.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if offer_items != blank or automated -%}
      {{ 'reco.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
      <script>
        window.EasyReco = window.EasyReco || {};
        window.EasyReco.offer = {
          productId: {{ product.id | json }},
          {%- comment -%}
            The offer type, which decides the injected layout: the card-style
            types render as a carousel of rows — one product at a time, the shape
            the offer editor previews — and the bundle types as a grid. Null for a
            list curated on the recommendations page, which never had a type.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The offer behind this data, when there is one. reco.js asks the app
            whether it is still live before rendering anything — a metafield cannot
            say whether the offer it mirrors still exists. `reco.offerId` is the
            per-product shape, `reco.id` the shop-list one; a list curated on the
            recommendations page has neither and renders on its own authority.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          offerId: {{ offer_id | json }},
          type: {%- if reco.type -%}{{ reco.type | json }}{%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
          copy: {%- if reco.copy -%}{{ reco.copy | json }}{%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
          render: {%- if reco.render -%}{{ reco.render | json }}{%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
          {%- comment -%}
            Automated offers carry the intent instead of a list: reco.js asks
            Shopify for `related` or `complementary` products in the browser, the
            same request the theme block's own Related source makes.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          source: {%- if reco.source -%}{{ reco.source | json }}{%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
          visibility: {%- if reco.visibility -%}{{ reco.visibility | json }}{%- else -%}null{%- endif -%},
          items: [{{ offer_items }}]
        };
        {%- comment -%}
          What is already in the cart, for the "hide products already in cart"
          filter. Emitted from Liquid because `cart` is only readable here — and
          only when an offer actually asked for the filter, since it is a list of
          the shopper's cart on the page for nothing otherwise.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if reco.visibility.hideInCart -%}
          window.EasyReco.cart = [
            {%- for line in cart.items -%}
              {%- unless forloop.first %},{% endunless -%}
              {{ line.product_id | json }}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ];
        {%- endif -%}
      </script>
      {%- comment -%}
        The runtime, loaded here and not only by the blocks.

        Blocks declare reco.js through their schema `javascript` key, so on a page
        with no block the script was never on the page at all and nothing injected
        the offer — which looked exactly like the embed being broken, and was
        fixed the same day it shipped.

        Loading it from both places is safe: reco.js bails immediately if it has
        already run (`window.EasyReco.loaded`), so the duplicate script tag a page
        with a block produces costs one cached request and nothing else.

        Only loaded when there is an offer to draw. A product with none needs
        nothing from this file.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <script src="{{ 'reco.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Recently viewed history.

  Recorded here because the embed runs on every page, whereas reco.js only
  loads where a block is placed — a shopper can browse a dozen products without
  ever hitting one. Handles only: the Recently viewed block re-fetches live
  product JSON, so nothing stored here can go stale.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if template.name == 'product' and product -%}
  <script>
    (function () {
      try {
        var KEY = 'easy-reco:recently-viewed';
        var MAX = 20;
        var handle = {{ product.handle | json }};
        var list = JSON.parse(window.localStorage.getItem(KEY) || '[]');
        if (!Array.isArray(list)) list = [];
        list = list.filter(function (item) {
          return typeof item === 'string' && item !== handle;
        });
        list.unshift(handle);
        window.localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(list.slice(0, MAX)));
      } catch (error) {
        // Private browsing, a full quota, or storage disabled — the block just
        // stays empty. Never break the page over a merchandising nicety.
      }
    })();
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Easy Recommendation",
  "target": "body",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_tracking",
      "label": "Track recommendation performance",
      "info": "Turn off to stop recording impressions, clicks and add to carts. Recommendations keep working.",
      "default": true
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
